# Offline / DTN operation

Design for **disconnected operation with opportunistic sync** as the default
mode, not as a degraded edge case. Physical site nouns (mine, vehicle, depot,
shift length) are **product / ODD** inputs — not constitution constants.

## (a) Tier sync by link quality

Where link quality varies by location or time:

| Tier | Carries | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **Thin / opportunistic** | Metrics, heartbeat, drift/health **flags** — tiny | Keeps fleet-view alive without shipping bulk |
| **Fat / high-quality** | **Bulk capture payloads** | Heavy training data drains only when the link can take it |

Heavy captures **wait for the fat tier by design** — do not force a shift of
raw inputs through a thin tier. Exact physical tiers and names are product
spec (connectivity profile / ODD).

## (b) Buffer sizing and degradation

- Size buffers from the product's declared **worst-case offline window**
  (ODD-derived), plus margin — not a hardcoded global constant.
- Buffer must be **disk-backed** and survive power-down between windows.
- On saturation: **evict by value** (see **`local-buffer-pattern.mdc`**);
  reservoir-sample heavy inputs so mid-window interesting events are not
  erased by routine bulk.

## (c) On-device autonomy during blackout

With zero connectivity, everything that must happen *during* the blackout
happens on device: drift/ODD detection that gates capture, buffer pressure
management, local health judgement for revert authority when product requires
**on-device-autonomous** revert. The cloud cannot be in the loop.

## (d) Control plane already survives this

Desired-state reconciliation (**`desired-state-reconciliation.mdc`**) means a device
offline for the whole window picks up the latest desired state at next
connect. Large artifact downloads defer to the fat tier — no special-case
command queue for coverage gaps.

